Thanks to Steve VanDevender, Oisin McGuinness, Bob Harris, Dr. Alan=20
Rollow, James Fitzgerald, Bob Vickers and B=E5rd Tesaker  for there help an=
d=20
time on explaining the behavior of my file domain. It appears that there=20
isn't a problem at all.

The behavior that I seeing from the /data5 filesystem is due to the way=20
that our version of oracle allocates space in it's temp files.
"Thomas, Thomas" provided this nugget of knowledge from the Oracle=20
webpage:
=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=
=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=
=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F
fact: Oracle Server - Enterprise Edition 8.1=20
fact: tempfile=20
symptom: space for a tempfile not allocated in the filesystem=20
symptom: space not completely used in filesystem=20
cause: A temporary tablespace with a tempfile is added to the database:=20
        create temporary tablespace test=5Ftemp TEMPFILE '/oracle/test=5Fte=
mp.dbf'=20
        size 400m reuse extent management local uniform size 1024k;=20
You see the correct filesize with a 'ls -l', but a df -k reports too much=20
free=20
space in that filesystem.=20
Tempfiles are not fully allocated and initialized during creation or=20
resize.=20
The description for this performance feature was omitted in Oracle8i.=20
fix:=20
The Oracle9i Database Concepts Release 1 guide has been updated to reflect =

this=20
behaviour.=20
For example:=20
Locally managed temporary tablespaces have temporary datafiles (tempfiles) =

which are similiar to ordinary datafiles except that:=20
When you create or resize tempfiles, they are not always guaranteed=20
allocation=20
of disk space for the filesize specified. On certain file systems (for=20
example=20
UNIX) disk blocks are allocated not at file creation or resizing, but=20
before the=20
blocks are accessed.=20
Caution: This enables fast tempfile creation and resizing;=20
        however the disk could run out of space later when the tempfiles ar=
e=20
        accessed.=20
=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=
=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=
=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F=5F
In essence our oracle temp files are "sparse" files as mentioned by=20
several people above. Sparse files are files that have a "hole" in them (a =

range of empty bytes). So for example:

# ls -ls TEMP01.dbf
197280 -rw-r-----   1 oracle   system   209723392 Mar 10 06:51 TEMP01.dbf
# du -kx ./*
197280  ./TEMP01.dbf
80      ./TEMP03.dbf
1       ./test.script
13896   ./test.test
# ls -l
total 211257
-rw-r-----   1 oracle   system   209723392 Mar 10 06:51 TEMP01.dbf
-rw-r-----   1 oracle   system   209723392 Mar  5 10:46 TEMP03.dbf
-rw-r--r--   1 root     system          69 Feb 23 08:09 test.script
-rw-r--r--   1 root     system    14226971 Mar  9 10:19 test.test
# pwd
/data5/u002/oradata/prod

The TEMP01.dbf file has 209723392 bytes allocated but only has 197280=20
bytes used. A  few weeks ago when I was seeing my file domain grow but=20
could not see any files growing, well the df, vdf, and showfdmn commands=20
show the file system space that is used. the ls command was displaying the =

space that we had allocated of which was not changing. The space was being =

used by oracle filling in the holes in the temp files (extents).

Thanks again for all of the quick responses and the time that I was given=20
from the people above. This case (for you HP folk: 3204932768) has been=20
worked by HP for about 3 weeks now and around six Technicians / Engineers=20
have tried to find the answer to my problem.=20

More info on sparse files can be found at:
www.cs.wisc.edu/~thain/library/sparse.pdf=20

-David Knight

