Original post is at the bottom.

Some suggested ideas/thoughts:

1.) Are the CPU revs up-to-date?
    <Yes>

2.) When you flashed the SRM firmware, did you also flash the ARC
    firmware?
    <Yes>

3.) Was serial console configured?
    <The console mode was set to "graphics" but as it turned out, when
    I attached a serial terminal, the arc was displayed there on the
    terminal.>

4.) If two graphics cards, remove one.   I only have one, but good idea.

5.) Dr. Tom wrote:
    One thing that AlphaBIOS is probably trying to do is run all of the
    IOS extensions on the adapter cards in the system.

    If one of them doesn't work, that might well hang the AlphaBIOS.

    I'd start pulling out options and putting them back one at a time
    until I found what's hanging the AlphaBIOS -- but then, it's not
    my system and I've never actually had to do that myself..

    <I never did get around to trying the above.>

6.) Dr. Tom wrote:
    That suggests to me that the ARC console (AlphaBIOS) can't find a
    compatible graphics interface.  If you have a generic SVGA type
    of card (something compatible with the graphics screen on the
    system), try plugging that in.

    Remember, the SRM console's environment variables mean nothing to
    the ARC/AlphaBIOS code -- it is doing the equivalent of a power on
    self test, probing for hardware; if it can't recognize a graphics
    card, it's going to use the serial console instead if it can find
    that..

    <Tom was getting close...>

Solution:  The graphics card was installed in one of the PCI-1 slots and
           not PCI-0, where is should have been.  IT WASN'T ME, I SWEAR!
           All is well now!

